## Why (rationale, verified 2026-08-01, re-verified 2026-08-02)

- **DeepSeek V4 Flash on the frontier** (re-routed 2026-08-02 after 0731 official release):
  DeepSeek shipped V4-Flash-0731 official on 2026-07-31 (re-post-trained, TB2.1 82.7,
  DeepSWE 54.4) with benchmarks "far exceeding V4-Pro-Preview" — while the V4-Pro
  API was NOT updated (still the Apr preview). Flash now beats Pro on strength AND
  price ($0.14/$0.28 vs $0.435/$0.87) AND pool ($60 vs $15/mo). The whole paid
  stack consolidates onto flash: frontier trio + planners (metis/momus, off
  minimax-m3) + worker/hephaestus/atlas (off mimo-v2.5 — same price, weaker).
  qwen3.7-plus kept at concurrency 3 as fallback/overflow. Qwen3.7 Max ($2.50/$7.50)
  stays budget-blocked — concurrency 0. Kimi K2.7 Code ($0.95/$4.00) stays demoted.
  Re-evaluate when V4-Pro official lands ('coming soon').
- **fallback_models allowlist (2026-08-03, claude-opus-5 incident)**: every agent/category carries
  `fallback_models: [opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash, opencode-go/gpt-5.6-luna, opencode-go/minimax-m3,
  opencode/deepseek-v4-flash-free, opencode/mimo-v2.5-free]` — suppresses OMO's HARDCODED
  AGENT_MODEL_REQUIREMENTS fallback chain (sisyphus chain[0] was claude-opus-5/anthropic —
  fired on transient errors despite disabled_providers, causing 'insufficient balance'
  mystery switches; see docs/INCIDENTS.md). Test 5 enforces this allowlist permanently.
- **GPT 5.6 Luna on code-gen**: budget frontier (SWE-Pro 62.7, coding rank #6,
  $0.20/$1.20 — 1/12 of Max's input price, 2,050 req/5h). **Luna is served via
  OpenAI `/responses`, not chat/completions** — works only through opencode's
  BUILT-IN opencode-go provider, which auto-selects transport per model.
- **MiniMax M3 only on visual-engineering**: the one slot that genuinely needs
  image/video input. Agentic numbers are vendor-only and BenchLM-independent ranks it
  weak (#96/128 agentic, 40.5/100); flash 0731 dominates (TB2.1 82.7 vs 66.0) at
  2.7× less. M3 supersedes minimax-m2.7. metis/momus moved to flash (text-only).
- **DeepSeek V4 Flash on main**: 31,650 req/5h effectively unlimited, $0.14,
  TB2.1 82.7 after the 0731 update — the workhorse.
- **mimo-v2.5-free on trivial**: free tier, ~30k req/5h, high-volume grep-style
  work must not bleed the shared $12/5h value pool that funds the frontier tier.
- **qwen3.7-plus over qwen3.6-plus**: 3.6-plus is superseded and has a known
  tool-call-loop bug. (qwen3.6-plus + minimax-m2.7 removed from
  modelConcurrency.)

## NOT-list (do not do)

1. **NEVER add a custom `opencode-go` provider entry with
   `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible`** — it breaks gpt-5.6-luna (responses-only
   transport). The built-in opencode-go provider handles per-model transport.
2. No Kimi K3 ($3/$15), no Grok 4.5 ($2/$6) — spend stays below that tier.
3. No GLM-5.2 on this platform ($1.40/$4.40 sandwiched; only earns its place
   self-hosting the MIT weights).
4. Routing scope only — no permission/hook/agent-definition/provider changes.
5. No non-opencode-go / non-deepseek providers.
6. No `opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro` — still the Apr preview; V4-Flash-0731 official dominates it on strength, price, and pool. Re-evaluate only when V4-Pro official ships.

## Budget layering

Three enforcement layers (deliberate, not a conflict): soft routing target ~$15/mo
(falsification trigger above) → hard block via routing + `modelConcurrency` caps
(per-model concurrency zeros out budget-blocked models; qwen3.7-max concurrency=0 since 2026-08-02)
→ monitoring ceiling $60/mo. Dead budget files `cost-guard.config.json` + `token-monitor.json` deleted 2026-08-02 (commit a8cb111).

## Refresh trigger

Lightweight periodic re-review (LANDSCAPE-lite): when the user senses the
landscape shifted, or a model is added/retired on opencode.ai/docs/go, or
falsification signals appear (frontier outputs no measurable gain ~2 weeks, main
agent 429-starvation, spend >$15/mo). Re-verify against the live model list
(https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1/models) and models.dev before changing anything.

## Benchmark integrity (2026-08-03)

When comparing models during LANDSCAPE checks, cite ONLY contamination-resistant
benches. **SWE-bench Verified is DEAD as a signal** — OpenAI Frontier Evals
stopped reporting it (Apr 2026): audit found >60% of tasks unsolvable-as-written,
models reproduce gold patches VERBATIM from task ID, saturated at 93.9%;
any score >80% warrants harness/tool-access scrutiny. Do NOT cite it.

Trustworthy: SWE-bench Pro (contamination-resistant by design — GPL-copyleft
+ private repos, held-out/commercial subsets; frontier ~59% on Pro vs 80-95%
on Verified), LiveCodeBench (time-split, contamination-free by design), HLE +
PaperBench (novel/fail-safe design). Caveat-only: Terminal-Bench 2.1 (canary
"largely symbolic safeguard" per its own paper; interactive-CLI harder to game
but vendor-reported numbers like flash 0731's 82.7 need independent confirm),
BenchLM (aggregate), NL2Repo/Cybergym/Toolathlon/DSBench/DeepSWE (vendor-reported).
